---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Shop Vacs",
    "url": "/best-shop-vac/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Shop Vac Attachments",
    "url": "/best-shop-vac-attachments/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Cordless Shop Vacs",
    "url": "/best-cordless-shop-vac/"
  },
  {
    "title": "What Size Shop Vac Do I Need?",
    "url": "/what-size-shop-vac-do-i-need/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Shop Vac CFM vs Horsepower",
    "url": "/shop-vac-cfm-vs-horsepower/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Shop Vac Bags",
    "url": "/best-shop-vac-bags/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-05", modified: "2026-11-05" });
const title = "7 Best Small Shop Vacs in 2026: Compact Wet/Dry Vacs for Cars, Apartments and Workshops";
const description =
  "The best small shop vacs from 1.5 to 5 gallons, ranked on listed CFM, sealed pressure, amps and hose size, plus wall-mount picks for garages and cars.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "ridgid-4000rv-50313",
    "name": "RIDGID 50313 Model 4000RV 4 Gallon 5.0 Peak HP Wet/Dry Vac",
    "category": "4 Gallon, 110 CFM, 1-7/8 in. Locking Hose",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "110 CFM rating, the highest airflow figure in this roundup",
      "1-7/8 in. locking hose, so it takes full-size RIDGID accessories",
      "20 ft cord reaches across a garage bay without an extension",
      "9 amp draw leaves room on a shared 15 amp circuit",
      "Polypropylene drum shrugs off dents and rust"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Around 14.5 to 15.7 lb depending on the listing, heavier than the 2.5 gallon units",
      "4 gallons of drum volume means emptying often on water jobs"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The small shop vac to buy if you only buy one: full-size airflow and hose in a 4 gallon body that fits on a shelf.",
    "description": "The 4000RV is RIDGID's compact 4 gallon wet/dry vac, sold under catalog number 50313, and it is the reason a small vac no longer has to feel small. Retailer spec sheets list 110 CFM of airflow from a 5.0 peak HP motor drawing 9 amps at 120V, which is the same airflow class as many 6 gallon units. The hose is the other half of the story: listings describe a 1-7/8 inch by 8 foot locking hose, so it accepts RIDGID's full-size nozzles and the 1-7/8 inch kits in our attachments guide rather than the toy-sized tools that come with 2.5 gallon vacs. The 20 foot cord is long for this class. Weight is listed at roughly 14.5 to 15.7 lb, so it is a one-hand carry, and the polypropylene drum will not rust if you leave water in it overnight. The trade-off is capacity: four gallons of drum volume is maybe three gallons of usable water before the float shuts it off. For cars, a basement corner and a workbench, nothing else here does as much per square foot of storage."
  },
  {
    "id": "dewalt-dxv04t",
    "name": "DEWALT DXV04T 4 Gallon 5.0 Peak HP Poly Wet/Dry Vac",
    "category": "4 Gallon Toolbox Style, 90 CFM, 1-7/8 in. Hose",
    "badge": "Best for Workshops",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Toolbox shape stores on a shelf or in a truck bed without tipping",
      "1-7/8 in. x 7 ft hose that collapses to about 2 ft for storage",
      "20 ft cord with an integrated wrap",
      "Onboard storage for hose, cord and tools",
      "Ships with a cartridge filter, foam filter and a disposable dust bag"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Retailers list about 90 CFM, below the RIDGID and Shop-Vac 4 gallon units",
      "Stretch hose loses some airflow when fully extended"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The compact vac for a workshop or a jobsite truck, where storage shape matters more than the last 15 CFM.",
    "description": "The DXV04T is DEWALT's 4 gallon corded wet/dry vac in a toolbox-style body: a flat top, a carry handle across it and storage built into the shell. Retailer spec sheets list a 5 peak HP motor, about 90 CFM of airflow and a 20 foot cord with an integrated cord wrap. The hose is a 1-7/8 inch stretch hose that DEWALT describes as running from about 2 feet collapsed to 7 feet extended, and every included tool uses the 1-7/8 inch connection. One parts list includes two extension wands, a crevice nozzle, a utility nozzle, a cartridge filter, a disposable dust bag and a foam filter, which means it can handle drywall dust on day one without a separate purchase. It also converts to a blower by moving the hose to the exhaust. The case for it over the RIDGID is shape: it sits flat on a shelf, slides behind a truck seat and does not roll away. The case against is airflow, where it gives up about 20 CFM on the spec sheet, and stretch hoses always cost a little suction when pulled to full length."
  },
  {
    "id": "shop-vac-5914411",
    "name": "Shop-Vac 5914411 4 Gallon 5.5 Peak HP SVX2 Wet/Dry Vac",
    "category": "4 Gallon, 105 CFM, 69 in. Sealed Pressure",
    "badge": "Strongest Suction",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Listed at 265 air watts and 69 in. of sealed pressure, the strongest lift here",
      "105 CFM airflow figure",
      "Doubles as a blower",
      "20 ft cord per retailer listings"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Draws about 11.6 to 11.8 amps, so it can trip a shared 15 amp circuit with other loads",
      "Hose diameter is described differently across listings, so check before buying accessories"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The small vac for wet work and packed debris, where sealed pressure matters more than airflow.",
    "description": "The 5914411 is Shop-Vac's 4 gallon unit built around its SVX2 motor, which Shop-Vac markets as stronger and longer-lived than its standard 5.5 peak HP motor. The spec numbers back up the claim on lift: one industrial distributor lists 105 CFM, 265 air watts and 69 inches of sealed pressure at 11.6 amps, and another lists 11.8 amps. Sealed pressure, also called water lift, is the figure that tells you how well a vac pulls water up a hose or drags wet sawdust out of a carpet, and 69 inches is well above the 40 to 53 inches the 1.5 and 2.5 gallon units in this list are rated for. The Amazon listing describes a 1.5 inch by 8 foot hose and a 20 foot cord. Hose diameter is where we would be careful: other listings describe it differently, so measure the hose cuff before ordering add-on tools. The amp draw is the other caution. At close to 12 amps it is fine on its own circuit, but in a garage where a compressor or space heater shares the 15 amp breaker, you will find the limit."
  },
  {
    "id": "vacmaster-vwmb508",
    "name": "Vacmaster VWMB508 1101 5 Gallon 5 Peak HP Remote Control Wall Mount Wet/Dry Vac",
    "category": "5 Gallon Wall Mount, 21 ft of 1-7/8 in. Hose, Remote Switch",
    "badge": "Best Wall Mount",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "21 ft of 1-7/8 in. hose (10 ft standard plus 11 ft flex) reaches a whole car from one spot",
      "On/off switch in the hose handle, so you never walk back to the vac",
      "Wall rack with a quick-release lever, so it also works as a portable",
      "20 ft cord and a 2-year limited warranty",
      "Vacmaster lists 68 to 69 in. of water lift and 155 air watts"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Vacmaster's own page lists 75 CFM in one place and 78 CFM in another",
      "26.7 lb, heavy to lift off the wall and carry around"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The one to buy if the vac is going to live on a garage wall and serve the car and the workbench from there.",
    "description": "The VWMB508 is Vacmaster's current 5 gallon wall-mount vac, the successor to the long-running VWM510. Vacmaster's spec sheet lists an 8 amp, 5 peak HP motor with 155 air watts and 68 inches of water lift, plus airflow of 75 CFM in the spec table and 78 CFM in the feature list, measured at the end of the included hose. The selling point is the setup rather than the motor. It hangs on a wall rack with a quick-release lever, and the hose is 21 feet in total: a 10 foot by 1-7/8 inch standard hose and an 11 foot flex hose. That is enough to reach all four doors and the trunk of a parked car from a vac mounted on the garage wall. The hose handle has a remote on/off switch, which is the feature owners commonly say they would not give up. Included tools cover a car nozzle, crevice tool, utility nozzle, concentrator nozzle and two extension wands, and filtration is a cartridge filter plus a foam wet filter. At 26.7 lb, it is a wall unit you can move, not a portable."
  },
  {
    "id": "shop-vac-9303511",
    "name": "Shop-Vac 9303511 2.5 Gallon 2.5 Peak HP Wet/Dry Vac",
    "category": "2.5 Gallon, 60 CFM, 1-1/4 in. Hose, Wall Mountable",
    "badge": "Best Compact Wall Mount",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Wall-mounted design in a 2.5 gallon body",
      "60 CFM rating, strong for this size",
      "8 amp draw is easy on any household circuit",
      "Blower function"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "1-1/4 in. x 4 ft hose is short for a car",
      "Cord length is listed as both 10 ft and 12 ft by different retailers"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A small wall-hung vac for a laundry room or a utility closet that will see spills and dust, not big jobs.",
    "description": "The 9303511 is Shop-Vac's 2.5 gallon, 2.5 peak HP unit, and the Amazon listing describes it as a wall-mounted design, which makes it the smallest wall-hung option here. Retailer spec sheets list 60 CFM of airflow, 53 inches of sealed pressure and 120 peak air watts at 8 amps. Those are good numbers for 2.5 gallons; the Armor All and Craftsman units this size are rated for 49 to 53 CFM. The hose is the familiar 1-1/4 inch by 4 foot type, which is right for a utility room and a little short for a car interior, so expect to carry the vac to the door rather than leave it on the floor. Retailers disagree on the cord, listing both 10 and 12 feet. It also blows, which is useful for clearing a dryer vent or a workbench. The case for it is the spot it fills: the vac that hangs by the washer or water heater and gets grabbed for a leak. If you want it to clean a car, buy the Vacmaster wall mount instead."
  },
  {
    "id": "armor-all-aa255",
    "name": "Armor All AA255 2.5 Gallon 2 Peak HP Wet/Dry Utility Shop Vacuum",
    "category": "2.5 Gallon, 53 CFM, 1-1/4 in. x 6 ft Hose",
    "badge": "Best Budget for Cars",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Usually one of the cheapest corded wet/dry vacs sold",
      "6 ft hose is longer than most 2.5 gallon units",
      "6 amp draw, the lightest load in this list",
      "Converts to a blower and ships with cloth and foam filters"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "53 CFM rating, half the airflow of the 4 gallon picks",
      "10 ft cord per one retailer, short for working around a car"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy it for occasional car cleanups and spilled cereal on a tight budget, and accept that it is not a workshop tool.",
    "description": "The AA255 is the Armor All branded 2.5 gallon wet/dry vac, sold through Vacmaster, and it has been a cheap car-and-apartment vac for years. Vacmaster lists it at 2 peak HP, 6 amps and 53 CFM. A retailer listing describes a 1-1/4 inch by 6 foot hose and a 10 foot cord. The 6 foot hose matters more than it sounds: most 2.5 gallon vacs ship with 4 or 5 feet, and the extra foot or two is the difference between reaching the back seat footwell from the door and climbing in. It converts to a blower, has an auto shut-off float for wet pickup and ships with a cloth dry filter and a foam wet filter. The limits are what you would expect for the price. At 53 CFM it will clear sand and crumbs off car carpet, but it struggles with packed pet hair and anything heavier than sawdust. The short cord means an extension cord for most driveways. As a cheap vac that lives in a closet, it is fine. As your only shop vac, it is not."
  },
  {
    "id": "vacmaster-vh105",
    "name": "Vacmaster VH105 1.5 Gallon 2 Peak HP Wet/Dry Vac",
    "category": "1.5 Gallon, 42 CFM, Under 5.5 lb",
    "badge": "Smallest",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "1.5 gallon body weighing about 4.6 to 5.25 lb",
      "Wall mountable with onboard accessory storage",
      "5 amp motor runs off any outlet",
      "2-year limited warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "42 CFM and 40 in. of water lift, the weakest ratings here",
      "6 ft cord and 4 ft hose keep it on a short leash"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The vac for an apartment closet or a boat: small enough to store anywhere, strong enough for spills and crumbs.",
    "description": "The VH105 is about as small as a corded wet/dry vac gets: a 1.5 gallon tank, about 6 quarts, in a unit that Vacmaster lists at 5.25 lb and some retailers list at 4.59 lb. Vacmaster rates the 2 peak HP, 5 amp motor at 42 CFM and 55 air watts, and one retailer lists 40 inches of water lift. The hose is 1-1/4 inch by 4 feet and the cord is 6 feet. It can hang on a wall and stores its accessories onboard. Those numbers make its job clear. It is for the spill under the sink, the cat litter that missed the box, a dorm room, an RV or a boat, where a 4 gallon vac has nowhere to live. It is not for a car interior, where 4 feet of hose and 6 feet of cord mean working with an extension cord and the vac on the seat. If you are choosing between this and a handheld cordless vacuum, the VH105 wins on wet pickup, which a stick or handheld cannot do, and loses on convenience. Owners commonly report it is louder than its size suggests."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Small Shop Vac",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-small-shop-vac/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best small shop vac?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people, the RIDGID 4000RV (catalog 50313). It is a 4 gallon vac listed at 110 CFM and 9 amps, with a 1-7/8 inch by 8 foot locking hose and a 20 foot cord. That is full-size airflow and accessory compatibility in a body that fits on a shelf. If it will hang on a garage wall, the Vacmaster VWMB508 is the better choice."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a 2.5 gallon shop vac big enough?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a car, spills and an apartment, yes, as long as you accept 50 to 60 CFM and a 1-1/4 inch hose. It will not handle wood chips, gravel or much water, because it holds about 1.5 gallons before the float closes. If you have the space for a 4 gallon vac, the jump in airflow is worth more than the extra size costs."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the smallest shop vac that is still useful?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The Vacmaster VH105 is about as small as useful gets: a 1.5 gallon tank, about 5 lb, 42 CFM and 40 inches of water lift. It handles spills, litter and crumbs in an apartment, RV or boat. Below that size you are into handheld territory, and handheld vacuums do not do wet pickup."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are mini shop vacs good for cars?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, with the right hose and tools. Look for at least 6 feet of hose and 15 to 20 feet of cord so the vac can stay outside the car. The RIDGID 4000RV and Vacmaster VWMB508 are the best car vacs here. A dedicated car attachment kit often improves the job more than a bigger motor."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a wall-mount shop vac worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In a garage, usually yes. It stays off the floor, stays plugged in so it gets used, and can carry a much longer hose. The Vacmaster VWMB508 has 21 feet of 1-7/8 inch hose and a remote switch in the handle, enough to clean a parked car from the wall. Mount it into studs at chest height so you can lift it off full."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many CFM should a small shop vac have?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Look for 90 CFM or more if it will handle garage floors, sawdust or a lot of car work; the 4 gallon picks here list 90 to 110 CFM. For spills and light cleanup, 40 to 60 CFM is fine. Check where the brand measures it: Vacmaster measures at the end of the hose, which gives lower numbers than brands that measure at the motor."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I buy a cordless or corded small shop vac?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Buy corded if the vac will mostly be used within reach of an outlet; it is cheaper, stronger and never stops for a recharge. Buy cordless if you work at the curb or in remote spots and already own batteries for that platform. Our cordless shop vac guide covers the battery models from Milwaukee, Makita, DEWALT and Craftsman."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "small shop vac",
      "mini shop vac",
      "compact wet dry vac",
      "small wet dry vac",
      "wall mount shop vac",
      "shop vac for car"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Small Shop Vacs</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          A 4 gallon vac with a 1-7/8 inch hose now moves as much air as many 6 gallon units. Here are the seven compact corded wet/dry vacs worth buying, from a 1.5 gallon closet vac to a 21 foot wall mount, and the spec-sheet numbers that separate them.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#ridgid-4000rv-50313"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Shaheer Ali Khan</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Consumer Product Testing &amp; Materials Performance Specialist</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-counts" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What Counts as Small</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The 7 Best, Ranked</a>
          <a href="#airflow" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Airflow vs Peak HP</a>
          <a href="#hose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Hose Diameter</a>
          <a href="#cars" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. For Car Detailing</a>
          <a href="#apartments" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Apartments and Storage</a>
          <a href="#wall-mount" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Wall-Mount Vacs</a>
          <a href="#workshop" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. In a Workshop</a>
          <a href="#water" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Water Pickup</a>
          <a href="#cordless" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Corded vs Cordless</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A small shop vac is the one that actually gets used. It lives on a shelf, a wall or in the trunk, it is plugged in within a minute, and it handles the spills, crumbs and sawdust that are not worth dragging a 16 gallon drum out for. The catch is that small vacs vary enormously: the 1.5 gallon units list around 40 CFM through a 1-1/4 inch hose, while the best 4 gallon units list 105 to 110 CFM through a 1-7/8 inch hose. This guide ranks the seven compact corded wet/dry vacs worth buying and shows you which numbers to trust on the box.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-counts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Counts as a Small Shop Vac
          </h2>

          <p>
            In this guide, a small shop vac means a corded wet/dry vac with a drum of 1 to 5 gallons. That covers three classes that do quite different jobs. The 1 to 2.5 gallon units, such as the Vacmaster VH105, Armor All AA255 and Shop-Vac 9303511, run on 5 to 8 amp motors and ship with 1-1/4 inch hoses. The 4 to 5 gallon units, such as the RIDGID 4000RV, DEWALT DXV04T, Shop-Vac 5914411 and Vacmaster VWMB508, run on 8 to 12 amp motors, mostly use 1-7/8 inch hoses and post airflow figures close to full-size vacs.
          </p>

          <p>
            The gallon figure is drum volume, not what you can collect. RIDGID says so in the small print on its own spec sheets, and it applies to every brand. The filter cage, the float and the inlet deflector take up space, so a 4 gallon vac holds roughly 3 gallons of water before the float closes, and a 2.5 gallon unit closer to 1.5. With dry debris, a filter bag or a fine dust cartridge takes up more room. Plan on emptying a small vac about twice as often as the drum size suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Why buy a small one at all? Three reasons come up over and over. You have nowhere to keep a 12 or 16 gallon drum, as in an apartment, a condo storage closet or a one-car garage that already holds a car. You want a vac to keep somewhere specific, such as in the car trunk, on a boat, by the water heater or under a workbench, so you are not dragging the big one out for a five-minute job. Or you need something you can carry up a ladder or into an attic with one hand.
          </p>

          <p>
            If none of those apply and you have the room, a 6 to 12 gallon vac costs only a little more and empties less often, and our main <a href="/best-shop-vac/">best shop vac guide</a> covers those. If you are still deciding on size, <a href="/what-size-shop-vac-do-i-need/">what size shop vac do I need</a> walks through tank, hose and airflow by job. This page assumes you already know you want something compact and corded, and it ranks the ones worth buying.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Four gallons with a 1-7/8 inch hose is the sweet spot.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Below 4 gallons, airflow drops to 40-60 CFM and hoses shrink to 1-1/4 inch. At 4 gallons you get 90-110 CFM and full-size accessories in a body that still fits on a shelf. Go smaller only when storage space truly forces it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

