        <div id="airflow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Airflow, Not Peak HP: Reading a Small Vac's Spec Sheet
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every vac on this list is sold on peak horsepower, and peak horsepower tells you almost nothing. It is a figure measured on a motor with the fan removed, driven to the point where it is about to stall, for an instant. Vacmaster's own fine print says peak HP does not denote the operational horsepower output of a wet/dry vac, and Shop-Vac and RIDGID carry similar footnotes. Craftsman's CMXEVBE17250, a 2.5 gallon vac we considered for this list, shows how loose the figure is: its own product page title says 1.75 peak HP, its description says 2.5 peak HP, and both describe the same machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            The useful numbers are airflow in CFM, sealed pressure (water lift) in inches, and amps. CFM tells you how much air moves through the hose, which is what picks up light debris like sawdust, dust and crumbs. Sealed pressure tells you how hard the vac can pull against a blocked nozzle, which is what lifts water up a hose and drags packed debris out of carpet. Amps tell you how much power the motor draws, and they are the most honest of the three because the label has to be accurate for electrical safety. Our deeper explainer on <a href="/shop-vac-cfm-vs-horsepower/">shop vac CFM vs horsepower</a> covers the physics; here is what the small vacs actually list.
          </p>

