        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy a 4 gallon vac with a 1-7/8 inch hose unless storage space truly forces you smaller. The RIDGID 4000RV is the best all-rounder at 110 CFM, the DEWALT DXV04T stores best in a workshop or truck, and the Shop-Vac 5914411 has the most lift for wet work. If the vac will live on a garage wall, the Vacmaster VWMB508's 21 feet of hose and remote switch make it the most convenient car vac here.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Go to 2.5 gallons or smaller only for a closet, a laundry room, an RV or a boat, and expect 40 to 60 CFM and a 1-1/4 inch hose. Whatever size you buy, ignore peak horsepower, read the CFM, sealed pressure and amps, and budget for a car kit or a cyclone separator. Those add-ons change what a small vac can do more than any motor upgrade.
          </p>
